  (:adr/id "adr-2609110500-a-parity-harness-found-the-oracles-own-two-branches-disagree")
  (:adr/topic "q9-whole-component-parity-and-session-close")
  (:adr/title "A parity harness found the oracle's own two reader-conditional branches disagree")
  (:adr/status "accepted")
  (:adr/date "2026-09-10")
  (:adr/deciders (vector "Jun Kawasaki" "Claude Opus 5"))
  (:adr/repos (vector "com-junkawasaki/root" "kotoba-lang/com-abb-robotics"))
  (:adr/related (vector "adr-2609110400-the-converters-reader-was-more-permissive-than-the-compilers"
                "adr-2609110200-a-moving-defcid-reaches-the-conformance-corpus-and-no-deployment-lock"))
  (:adr/body
  "## What this closes

`com-abb-robotics` carries the third whole-component build. Its own
migration-record.edn listed, under `:not-claimed`, that the `oracle-*`
functions existed and were RUN and their values discriminated -- but had never
been compared value-by-value against `main.cljc`. Running a thing and agreeing
with the thing it replaces are two different claims, and only the second is a
port. `q9-oracle.cljs` now exists in that repository (merged 8641d0b1) and
closes it.

## Reproduce

    # the CLJC side
    nbb --classpath src:<kotoba-lang/text>/src q9-oracle.cljs

    # the artifact side
    amu check   src/abb_robotics/whole_component.kotoba --jvm-free --policy policy.edn
    amu compile src/abb_robotics/whole_component.kotoba --target aarch64-macos \\
                --policy policy.edn --output abb.kexe
    amu extract-native abb.kexe --symbol <oracle-name> --output s.bin   # prints :offset
    KEXE_FUEL=100000000 kexe-loader s.bin <offset> <arity> aarch64 7,23 [selector]

Both sides were RUN in this pass. `amu check` returned `:ok true`, `amu
compile` returned `:ok true` at 237845 bytes, and every number below came out
of the executed artifact rather than a build log.

## What agrees, and what does not

Agreeing: route count 30 on both sides -- computed from the entity table rather
than written down; nine of nine `as-bool` selectors; five of six `as-int`.

Disagreeing, on exactly one input: `as-int` of `\"12abc\"` is 0 from the artifact
and 12 from the oracle run under nbb.

**It is not a port defect, and finding that out is what the harness bought.**
The oracle's `as-int` is a reader conditional whose two branches disagree with
each other on this input:

    :cljs   js/parseInt(\"12abc\", 10)      -> 12   (takes the leading digits)
    :clj    Long/parseLong(\"12abc\")       -> throws, caught, -> 0

Both were RUN rather than read. `nbb` printed 12; `clojure -M -e` printed 0.
The component matches the `:clj` branch.

So `parity with the oracle` is not a well defined property for this input. The
same harness reports a mismatch under nbb and a match on the JVM, and which one
is a bug depends on a decision nobody has made. It is recorded rather than
resolved: choosing a normative branch is a semantic decision about the product,
not a migration step.

This is worth generalising. A `.cljc` oracle is not one program. Where its
branches diverge, a port cannot be `faithful` to it -- it can only be faithful
to one branch, and the migration owes an explicit choice.

## A method note about what was compared

The expand layer is compared as a COUNT of the keys the fold added, not as a
hash of the rendered record. The `.kotoba` hashes a rendering, and the
rendering is this port's own convention, so a hash comparison there would
confound a behaviour difference with a layout difference. Counting asks the
question the two-ref fold is actually for: did it run once, twice, or not at
all.

## Session state at close

Landed to default branches this session, all verified present on `main`:

  - kotoba `c146f3df`  adl convert: consumer guards ask whether a Clojure
    consumer REMAINS; conversion set settled as a fixpoint
  - kotoba `47aa4de0`  adl convert: refuse what amu's source reader cannot
    read, and name the construct
  - com-abb-robotics `8641d0b1`  the parity harness and this finding
  - amu `#838` merged (dependabot, 13/13 green). `#837` then conflicted on the
    same lock file and is left for dependabot to rebase.
  - ADRs 2609110000, 2609110100, 2609110200, 2609110300, 2609110400 and this
    one. West pins advanced for `kotoba` and `com-abb-robotics`.

## Resume point

**The rename**: 824 repositories landed. 90 remain FAIL, and they are two
different problems: about 85 have no branch on the remote, so they need the
rename RE-RUN rather than a pull request; the rest are archived repositories,
which are a category and not a failure. The PR recovery finished cleanly --
110 recovered, 0 still rate-limited.

**The converter**: convert is not compile. 30 landed `.kotoba` files from 30
distinct repositories were put through `amu check` and none passed, while a
real Kotoba module passed as the control. The two dominant rejections are a
language gap -- dynamic projection of a map constant, `(get catalog k)` -- and
reader constructs. Neither is a converter defect.

**Next concrete step for Q9**: `com-aadhaar` and `com-aave` have `q9-oracle.cljs`
and now so does `com-abb-robotics`. Run the same harness against the other two
and see whether the `as-int` divergence reproduces there -- both are ports of
the same template, so if it does, the reader-conditional question is a cohort
decision rather than a repository one.

⚠ Left in place deliberately, not cleaned: 21 `wt-*` worktrees under the
session scratchpad from earlier agent runs. They are not this session's to
delete; `nbb scripts/worktree-retire.cljs --root . --apply` is the sanctioned
route and refuses anything dirty or in use.")))
